512056e-06,-0.00010990492592100054,0.0,-0.007294568233191967,-0.10821261256933212,-5.483612312673358e-06,0.0,-5.960462772236497e-07,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-0.003115326166152954,-3.4570634852570947e-06,-2.50339189733495e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,279,9904,311,198,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,59,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,15940,220,17,87,59,60,258,279,9873,400,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,1124,2493,2418,271,37314,9960,510,27,37420,91339,397,1249,11625,279,23606,17767,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,59,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,15940,220,17,87,57758,304,279,9873,17767,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,59,2493,59,701,582,1191,553,1667,279,1990,34381,9569,369,57668,11,1124,11520,15940,220,17,87,284,220,17,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,59,568,3719,3696,10607,419,1119,279,23606,11,582,633,510,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,59,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,50853,220,17,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,50,6383,7766,279,1290,24413,3108,11,582,614,510,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,18,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,5847,11,582,646,8168,700,1124,11520,15940,856,57758,504,2176,11067,315,279,23606,510,59,26056,15940,856,320,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,8,284,481,18,1124,15940,856,1124,9407,856,7110,921,1654,646,55327,844,419,311,510,59,26056,15940,856,320,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,488,220,18,1124,9407,856,8,284,220,15,7110,921,1986,23606,6696,601,1378,5048,311,2908,510,16,13,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,59,1326,17,13,17767,17,1124,15940,61,17,856,481,220,18,488,220,18,1124,9407,856,284,220,15,59,3593,334,4207,220,16,25,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,59,568,1019,785,9904,311,1124,11520,15940,856,284,220,15,57758,304,279,9873,17767,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,59,2493,57758,525,17767,87,284,220,15,11,1124,2493,11,220,17,59,2493,59,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of the solutions to\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2 \\pi.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we start by using the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nNext, we can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nWe can rearrange this to:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\nThis equation gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\),\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\).**\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"fefd670c1988b90d8d5c4122c539f1a060ad65667e52c488d500914c630c2ce9"},"seconds":58.6084201335907,"seed":51905512160401032,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we start by using the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nNext, we can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this to:\n\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\n\nThis equation gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\),\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\).**\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).**\nWe use the identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\), so the equation becomes:\n\n\\[2 (1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\n\nMultiplying through by -1, we have:\n\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(a \\cos^2 x + b \\cos x + c = 0\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -3\\), and \\(c = 1\\):\n\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are:\n\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{3 + 1}{4} = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos x = \\frac{3 - 1}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is \\(x = 0\\).\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) and \\(x = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nCombining all solutions from both cases, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe sum of these solutions is:\n\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi.\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5\\pi}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5\\pi}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we start by using the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nNext, we can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this to:\n\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x) = 0.\\]\n\nThis equation gives us two cases to consider:\n1. \\(\\sin x = 0\\),\n2. \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(\\sin x = 0\\).**\nThe solutions to \\(\\sin x = 0\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(2 \\sin^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0\\).**\nWe use the identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\), so the equation becomes:\n\n\\[2 (1 - \\cos^2 x) - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0.\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x - 3 + 3 \\cos x = 0,\\]\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\n\nMultiplying through by -1, we have:\n\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(a \\cos^2 x + b \\cos x + c = 0\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -3\\), and \\(c = 1\\):\n\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are:\n\n\\[\\cos x = \\frac{3 + 1}{4} = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos x = \\frac{3 - 1}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = 1\\), the solution in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) is \\(x = 0\\).\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), the solutions in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\) are \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) and \\(x = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nCombining all solutions from both cases, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe sum of these solutions is:\n\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 5\\pi.\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{5\\pi}\\).","id":"precalculus:64"}
